# Documentation Standards

Contents: Posture | Upkeep | Status accuracy | Verification | Style | Diagrams | Security claims | ADRs

## Posture

- Write for a technical reviewer judging repository credibility. Accuracy and stated limitations beat polish.
- Code, workflows, and configuration win over docs.
- Make small, targeted edits. Delete or narrow a wrong claim rather than rewriting the section.
- Do not rewrite for style alone or expand into adjacent sections.

## Upkeep

- A behavior change updates the README, manual, and `CHANGELOG.md` `[Unreleased]` in the same PR.
- Fix a nearby stale claim when it is local and verifiable.

## Status accuracy

- Lead with what works today. Keep limitations next to capabilities.
- Label planned, placeholder, and experimental behavior. Never describe roadmap items as shipped.
- Labels: Implemented, Placeholder, Planned, Experimental, Unknown.

## Verification

- CLI examples: check `src/cli/mod.rs` or run them.
- SQL examples: use columns from `src/lakehouse/schema.rs` and the FQN `iceberg.anti_entropator.file_catalog`.
- Workflow, coverage, audit, release, and container claims: match `.github/workflows/*`.
- Docker and deployment claims: match `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `docs/security/*`.
- Do not claim a test, scan, or audit passed unless it ran; otherwise write "not run".

## Style

- One document, one mode: tutorial, how-to, reference, or explanation. Do not mix modes; split and link.
- Lead with the fact, command, or limitation. No filler openers.
- Use the real symbol, file, flag, or command name. Call each thing by one name everywhere.
- Put the condition before the instruction. One instruction per sentence.
- Be specific: "a column rename fails the build", not "schema changes can cause issues".
- No performance or security claims without a measurement or an existing doc.
- Avoid "simply", "just", "robust", "seamless", "enterprise-grade", "production-ready". Say the behavior or the deployment profile.
- Use relative links for repository docs. Keep heading levels sequential.
- Quick-start commands must work on a fresh clone.
- Rust examples use `?` with `anyhow::Result`, semantic names (`landing_zone`), and no `.unwrap()`.
- Bold a core technology on first mention only when editing nearby text.

## Diagrams

- Label each `current`, `target`, or `current with planned elements`.
- Keep planned items (per-stage tracing, maintenance commands, full SQL REPL) out of current diagrams.
- Never put `dataflow-rs` or `--engine` in a diagram.
- Roles match `.cursor/rules/project-architecture.mdc`. Prefer short-label Mermaid.

## Security and delivery claims

- Label controls `enforced today`, `required for shared/public deployment`, or `planned`.
- `.env` is a local convenience, not a security boundary. No secrets or real credentials in docs.
- Compose ports are localhost-bound by default. Blue/green is a reference simulation.
- State when SBOM, provenance, scanning, or release gating is disabled or planned.

## ADRs

- Shape: Context, Decision, Current State, Consequences, Alternatives, References. Include a `Status:` line (`accepted`, `accepted, not yet implemented`, `superseded on <date>`).
- Preserve history. Add a dated Current State note for stale commands, schema, ports, or tables.
- Verify and date external facts. No star counts, download numbers, or vendor benchmarks.
- Point to the code module instead of copying schemas.
